  "Question": "Let \\(n\\) be the number of real values of \\(p\\) for which the roots of\n\n\\[ x^2-px+p=0 \\]\n\nare equal. Then \\(n\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "a finite number greater than \\(2\\)",
    "E": "an infinitely large number"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A quadratic has equal roots exactly when its discriminant is zero. Here the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is always \\(1\\), so the equation remains quadratic for every real \\(p\\). Its discriminant is\n\\[\n\\Delta=(-p)^2-4(1)(p)=p^2-4p.\n\\]\nThus equal roots require\n\\[\np^2-4p=p(p-4)=0,\n\\]\nwhich gives \\(p=0\\) or \\(p=4\\), with no other possibilities.\n\nBoth values work: \\(p=0\\) gives \\(x^2=0\\), with repeated root \\(0\\), and \\(p=4\\) gives \\((x-2)^2=0\\), with repeated root \\(2\\). These are two distinct parameter values, so \\(\\boxed{n=2}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
